From ebf5832457aaf4b3402dd4e7bdde2dc2ca57353e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BananaDS <bananads@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:19:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] ARMJIT_A64: the ARM9's slice ends inside a block chain
 when the loop would only keep time

When the ARM9's exit stub (ExitCheck, ExitCheckPC, DynExit, DynExitA, a self loop's inline check) finds
Timestamp >= Target with StopExecution clear, it went back to RunSlicesJIT through ARM_Ret: twelve registers
restored, the loop's iteration (the ARM9's time, GPU3D's idle case, the timers, the halted ARM7, RunSystem's
early-out, NextTarget), then the block lookup and ARM_Dispatch's twelve registers saved again. With the ARM7
halted (a game's usual state: it waits for IRQs) all the loop does there is keep time.

Now the stub calls NDS::SliceChain9, which makes the loop's own checks in its order: no event or timer due by
the ARM9's time, no GX stall, DMA or sleep, the geometry engine idle (GPU3D::Run's early-out), the ARM7 ahead or
halted with no IRQ to wake it, Running, the ARM9 not halted. If they hold it does the iteration's bookkeeping
(GPU3D's time, the ARM7's time and target, SysTimestamp) and NextTarget's ARM9Target, and the chain goes on into
the exit's next block; otherwise it changes nothing and the stub returns to the loop as before. The slice ends
stay where they were (every 64 cycles, at block ends), so the emulation is the same.

Only with FastLoop and FastSched (RunSlicesJIT) and the new code generation; MELONDS_JIT_SLICECHAIN=0 turns it
off. cpubench thumb under qemu: main thread 4,896k -> 4,696k instructions a frame (the loop 962k -> 352k,
ARM_Dispatch/ARM_Ret 192k -> 45k, SliceChain9 +510k, stubs +48k).
